WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:10.600
Persistence analysis on macOS focuses on programs, scripts, agents, daemons, profiles, and settings that cause software to run automatically or maintain a foothold.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.700
LaunchAgents and LaunchDaemons are key locations.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.200
LaunchAgents typically run in a user context, while LaunchDaemons usually run at a system level.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:25.340
Their plist files can describe labels, program paths, arguments, run conditions, and timing.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.360
Login items are another startup-related source.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.980
They may represent applications or helpers configured to launch when a user signs in.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.080
Cron, scheduled tasks, application-specific updaters, and shell startup files can also matter at a high level.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:48.060
In managed environments, configuration profiles and management tools may enforce settings, install agents, or configure security controls.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.560
Suspicious scripts or binaries should be interpreted defensively.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:01:01.140
A launch item pointing to an unusual path, a recently modified script, a binary outside expected application locations, or an unexpected helper tool may be a lead.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.840
It may also be legitimate software, an admin tool, or a vendor component.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:14.060
The analyst should compare the item with installation history, signatures, file metadata, logs, user context, and endpoint telemetry.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.640
The course focus is defensive persistence analysis only.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.340
Do not create or test persistence mechanisms outside authorized lab procedures.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:33.440
In real investigations, the goal is to identify what is configured to run, determine whether it fits the system role, preserve supporting evidence, and communicate confidence and limitations.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:39.020
Startup artifacts are most useful when placed in a timeline with user activity and system events.
